 "result": "I couldn't get the answer. Every query I ran failed with `database is locked. Try again.`, including a trivial `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders`, so the problem is with the database connection, not the query.\n\nI retried the order lookup 11 times over the session, with the same query and with simpler variants, and every attempt failed the same way. I stopped there rather than loop indefinitely. The lock hasn't cleared, so it probably needs something outside my reach, such as another process holding the database open.\n\nThe query I'd run once the lock clears is:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.item, o.total_eur, o.ordered_on, c.name\nFROM orders o\nLEFT JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.ordered_on < '2026-10-01'\nORDER BY o.ordered_on DESC, o.id DESC\nLIMIT 5;\n```\n\nThis assumes `ordered_on` is stored in ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` format. I haven't been able to confirm that yet. If the lock clears, I can rerun it right away. If it doesn't, check whether another process has the database file open, then tell me to try again.",
